Bridge 0131 (G7): fast YCbCr conversion of video surfaces. A 1080p NV12 frame took ~45-60 ms GPU through the staging copy of both
planes and a kernel loading 18 storage-buffer words a texel (0128's bake cut 35 %: latency-bound). For 8-bit 4:2:0 textures (420v/420f,
YCBCR8_420_2P and its _sRGB twin) the conversion now fetches the planes in place: an R8G8_UINT uniform texel buffer view over the
surface's own VkBuffer (no staging copy), one invocation per chroma pair writing its 2x2 texels from the 3x3 pairs around it (11 fetches
for 4 texels), the shape baked in (at most 8 shapes a process, as 0128). Same integer arithmetic: bit-exact with the old kernel (tests on
the CPU interpreter, every range/matrix, odd sizes, view offsets). Image format unchanged (RGBA16F / RGBA32F): the texture's views and
consumers stay as they are. Falls back to the old path for other layouts, plane-1 textures, a view past the span or the device's
limits, a rebound VkBuffer, or past 8 shapes. 0128's seed gating unchanged. Reuse: the binding (R8G8_UINT uniform texel buffer over the
planes' bytes, element-unit offsets) from upstream metal2vulkan cdf59197 bind/ycbcr_in_place.rs; not a1f16771/2f033d30's
VkSamplerYcbcrConversion route (needs samplerYcbcrConversion enabled at device creation, immutable-sampler layouts and a multi-planar
image over imported memory; those two still copy the planes into an optimal image). OwnedKernel gains uniform texel buffer bindings.
Off: SWEET_YCBCR_FAST=0. Log: "YCBCR FAST: WxH Converted in place (base N)" or "... kept the staging copy: why" (first 8).

